Z||000000||beachbomb||05-21-2000||07:28 PM||||HI again, another question about the 1984 Indy Fiero i just bought about a month ago! How hard would it be to take the 4 cylinder out and put a fiero V-6 in? Is there alot of modifications to make to the car, can i still use my 4 speed manual transmission? Please let me know, thanks..jim<BR>p.s. anyone know where the Tachometer filter is located on an 1984 4 cylinder?||||reg||5
Z||000001||GT Bastard||05-21-2000||08:09 PM||||You might want to think about the value of your car. It's an Indy, and there weren't many produced.  If you hack it up, you'll destroy it's value, and more than likely regret it later.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000002||redfiero||05-21-2000||08:14 PM||||My understanding is that the 4 to 6 swap in an 84 is very hard.  The 84 didn't have a 6 cylinder option therefore no thought was given to protecting for it.  Personally, I'd keep it a stock Indy and buy another.  One Fiero is never enough...can someone explain that to my wife?!?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000003||ounmongk||05-21-2000||08:21 PM||||welcome to the forum... like Mr. Bastard said previously, you have on your hands a collector's item; and most collectors want the item in the original shape: the way it came. So just keep yours running without modifying it... or if you really must do modifications, we can arrange a little car swap. ( at your convenience, of course) again, welcome to forum  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||GT Bastard||05-21-2000||08:24 PM||||It would also require a cradle from an '85 or later to accomadate the V6.  Not to mention loads of other crap.||||reg||1
